## Solution

We evaluate
$$I(a)=\int_{0}^{\pi}\frac{\ln(1+a\cos x)}{\cos x}\,dx,\qquad |a|<1.$$

**Step 1. Differentiate under the integral sign.**

For $|a|<1$ the integrand is regular (near $x=\pi/2$, $\frac{\ln(1+a\cos x)}{\cos x}\to a$), so we may differentiate w.r.t. $a$:
$$I'(a)=\int_{0}^{\pi}\frac{1}{1+a\cos x}\,dx.$$

**Step 2. Evaluate the standard integral.**

Use the Weierstrass substitution $t=\tan(x/2)$, so $\cos x=\frac{1-t^{2}}{1+t^{2}}$, $dx=\frac{2\,dt}{1+t^{2}}$, with $x:0\to\pi$ giving $t:0\to\infty$:
$$
\int_{0}^{\pi}\frac{dx}{1+a\cos x}
=\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{2\,dt}{(1+a)+(1-a)t^{2}}
=\frac{2}{1+a}\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{dt}{1+\frac{1-a}{1+a}t^{2}}.
$$
With $u=t\sqrt{\frac{1-a}{1+a}}$,
$$=\frac{2}{1+a}\sqrt{\frac{1+a}{1-a}}\cdot\frac{\pi}{2}=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{1-a^{2}}}.$$

Hence
$$I'(a)=\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{1-a^{2}}}.$$

**Step 3. Integrate back, using $I(0)=0$.**

$$I(a)=I(0)+\int_{0}^{a}\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{1-t^{2}}}\,dt=0+\pi\arcsin a.$$

**Result.**

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\int_{0}^{\pi}\frac{\ln(1+a\cos x)}{\cos x}\,dx=\pi\arcsin a,\qquad |a|\le 1.}$$

(At $a=\pm1$ the value follows by continuity, giving $\pm\pi^{2}/2$.)

